```
That's in shared/fence.ts. Then in mention.ts:

```ts
/** An inline code span: a run of backticks, its content, and the same run closing it. */
const INLINE_CODE = /(`+)[^`][\s\S]*?\1|`/  ...
```

Hmm, CommonMark inline code spans: a run of N backticks opens, and closes with a run of exactly N backticks. Content may contain backticks of other lengths. Let me implement simply:

```ts
const INLINE_CODE = /(`+)[\s\S]*?\1/g;
```

Wait that's not exactly right but the current implementation is a simple `` `[^`\n]*` `` which doesn't even allow multiple backticks. Being careful not to over-engineer: I should preserve the current behavior for inline spans (single backtick, no newline) to not break things. Actually the current CODE regex is: /```[\s\S]*?```|`[^`\n]*`/g. Inline span = single backtick pair with no backtick or newline between.

If I split fences out first, then within prose apply `/`[^`\n]*`/g`. That preserves inline behavior exactly. Keep it that way — minimal, preserves behavior.

So:

```ts
/** An inline code span: single backticks, no backtick or newline between. */
const INLINE_CODE = /`[^`\n]*`/g;

/** `text` in alternating runs of prose and code: fenced blocks first, then inline spans. */
function codeSegments(text: string): TextSegment[] {
  return fenceSegments(text).flatMap((segment) =>
    segment.code ? [segment] : inlineSegments(segment.text),
  );
}
```

And inlineSegments splits a prose string into segments. Let me write it with matchAll.

Now escapeUnknownMentions:

```ts
export function escapeUnknownMentions(text: string, known: Iterable<string>): MentionCheck {
  const allowed = new Set([...known].map((login) => login.trim().toLowerCase()).filter(Boolean));
  const escaped: string[] = [];

  const transform = (segment: string): string =>
    segment.replace(MENTION, (whole, before: string, login: string) => {
      if (allowed.has(login.toLowerCase())) return whole;
      if (!escaped.includes(login)) escaped.push(login);
      return `${before}\`@${login}\``;
    });

  const out = codeSegments(text)
    .map((segment) => (segment.code ? segment.text : transform(segment.text)))
    .join("");

  return { text: out, escaped };
}
```

