Now, let me verify the bug quickly with a repro, and check what the inline code span handling should be. Important: the current CODE regex handles both fenced blocks AND inline code spans. fenceSegments only handles fenced blocks (three or more backticks / tildes on their own line). So moving to fenceSegments alone would lose inline code span protection. Note the test at line 49: "an inline code span is left alone too" — `run \`npm i @scope/pkg\` then ask @stranger`. Actually, does `npm i @scope/pkg` get matched by MENTION? `@scope/pkg` — MENTION has `(?!\/)` trailing so `@scope/` is excluded. Hmm, so that test would still pass without inline code handling? Let's check: MENTION = `(^|[^\w@/-])@(login)\b(?!\/)`. In "`npm i @scope/pkg`", before `@` is a space → `[^\w@/-]` matches space. login = "scope", followed by `/` → `(?!\/)` fails. So not matched. Test passes but not for the reason stated.

Test line 55: "an already escaped mention is not escaped twice": `see \`@stranger\` above` → if inline code isn't skipped: transform on "see `" ... the `@stranger` preceded by backtick which is `[^\w@/-]` → matched, escaped → becomes `` `@stranger` `` → already wrapped gives `` see `` `@stranger` `` above ``. Actually it would produce "see ``@stranger`` above" which != "see `@stranger` above". So the test would fail. So we DO need inline code span handling.

So the fix: keep inline code span detection, plus use fenceSegments for fenced blocks. Let me think about how to compose.

