Wait — mention.ts's doc comment is at the top of the file (module doc). Placing an import after it is fine. But hold on: is that the module header before any code? Yes, lines 1-34 are the header comment. OK.

Now replace CODE usage.

Actually, let me reconsider the design. `escapeUnknownMentions` needs to transform segments. With codeSegments, I can do:

```ts
export function escapeUnknownMentions(text: string, known: Iterable<string>): MentionCheck {
  const allowed = ...;
  const escaped: string[] = [];
  const transform = (segment) => segment.replace(MENTION, ...);
  const out = codeSegments(text).map((s) => (s.code ? s.text : transform(s.text))).join("");
  return { text: out, escaped };
}
```

And mentionsIn:
```ts
const prose = codeSegments(text).filter(s => !s.code).map(s=>s.text).join(" ");
```
Hmm — the original replaced CODE with " " exactly (a space). Joining prose segments with " " preserves that property: prose on either side of code doesn't join. But wait — joining with " " adds a space even when the segment boundary is mid-line, e.g. "see `x`@baz" → prose segments "see " and "@baz", joined = "see  @baz". MENTION matches "@baz" — fine. And "`x`@baz" test expects ["baz"]. Good.

But careful: original code replaced CODE with a single space, so "a`` `x` ``b" → "a b"? Actually original: text.slice around. Fine.

Hmm, but joining with " " changes... consider "foo" code "bar" — "foo bar" vs original same. Fine.

Actually one subtle difference: with codeSegments, adjacent prose segments are split by the code span exactly, and joining with a space is equivalent to the original `.replace(CODE," ")` for the fenced case too. Good.

Let me keep mentionsIn simple.

Also the MENTION regex with "g" flag used with .replace — fine.

Note: escapeUnknownMentions currently does a weird `CODE.lastIndex = 0;` and matchAll. Simplify.

Also `MENTION` is a module-level regex with /g used in `mentionsIn` via `new RegExp(MENTION.source, "g")` — keep.

Let me write the new implementation.

